namespace Linx {

/**

\addtogroup random

\section random-intro Introduction

Random number generation is essential for simulations, stochastic algorithms, and data augmentation.
Linx provides a suite of random number generators that can be used to produce pseudo-random numbers and arrays,
according to various statistical distributions.
Compatibility with pixelwise operations simplifies the integration of randomness into existing workflows.

\section random-sampling Random number generation

Random number generators are provided to sample random values according to different distributions.
Generators offer a call operator which returns a pseudo-random value:

```cpp
auto rng = Linx::UniformRng(0., 1.);
auto r = rng(); // Sample a random float in the range [0, 1)
```

All generators are listed at the bottom of this page.

\section random-generation Random noise generation

Generators' call operator is a variadic method which discards its input parameter(s).
This seamingly weird behavior allows to use them seamlessly with pixelwise services.
For example, to generate an array of iid. uniform random noise in the range [0, 1), do:

```cpp
a.generate(Linx::UniformRng(0., 1.)); // In place
auto b = Linx::generate<float, 100>("noise", Linx::UniformRng(0., 1.)); // New instance
```

To add some Gaussian noise with mean 0 and standard deviation 1, simply write:

```cpp
a += Linx::GaussianRng(0., 1.);
```

*/

}
